# How to Use This Document

## What this topic covers

Topic 4 is syllabus **Head V — Gender and Development**. It is the **heaviest topic in the paper**: ==17.5 visits== in eleven papers, and at least one question **every year** from 2016 to 2026. It has four syllabus lines:

1. **colonial and capitalist perspectives** of gender;
2. a **gender analysis of development theories** — modernisation, world-system, dependency and structural functionalism;
3. the **WID, WAD and GAD** approaches, and the gender critique of **structural adjustment**;
4. **globalisation** and gender.

The examiner has added two newer lines: **climate change** (2026) and the claim that "**development is the new name of coloniality**" (2024). This document teaches all of them, with the global goals (MDGs and SDGs) as a final chapter.

::: simple
**Development** means making people's lives better — more income, health, education, freedom. **Gender and Development** asks a simple question: **better for whom?** A new road, a new factory or a loan from the IMF can help men and harm women, or the other way round. This topic teaches you how to see the difference.
:::

## How this document is built

1. **Part Zero** gives you the words of the topic, the question "development for whom?", and the whole topic on one diagram.
2. **Part One** has fifteen chapters: development itself (1); the colonial and capitalist perspectives (2–3); four development theories and a comparison (4–8); WID, WAD and GAD (9–11); structural adjustment (12); globalisation (13); climate (14); and the global goals (15).
3. **Part Two** sets out today's debates.
4. **Part Three** lists every past question, what the record shows, and five likely questions.
5. **Part Four** is a bank of about 125 numbered one-liners, followed by every past MCQ on the topic.
6. **Part Five** is the revision sheet — the whole topic in a few pages, every word explained.

# Part Zero — The ABC of Topic 4

## 0.1  The words of this topic

::: table | The key terms of Topic 4 (★ = you must be able to define it in one sentence).
@widths 3.6,10.4
Term | Simple meaning, with an example
Development ★ | Improvement in people's lives — income, health, education, freedom and choice. *Not only GDP growth.*
GDP | Gross Domestic Product — the value of goods and services produced in a country in a year. It **ignores unpaid work**.
Human development | Widening people's **choices and capabilities** (Mahbub ul Haq and Amartya Sen; UNDP Human Development Report, ==1990==).
Colonialism ★ | Rule of one country over another, for the ruler's benefit. *British rule in India, 1757/1858–1947.*
Coloniality | The **patterns of power** created by colonialism that continue after independence — in knowledge, economy and gender (Quijano, Lugones).
Capitalism ★ | An economy of private ownership, markets and wage labour, driven by profit.
Modernisation theory ★ | Poor countries develop by becoming like the West — industry, cities, "modern" values (Rostow, ==1960==).
Dependency theory ★ | Poor countries are **kept** poor by rich ones through unequal trade (Frank, Prebisch).
World-system theory ★ | The world economy is one system of **core, semi-periphery and periphery** (Wallerstein, ==1974==).
Structural functionalism ★ | Society is a system of parts that keep it **stable**; men's and women's roles are "functional" (Parsons).
WID ★ | **Women in Development** (1970s): bring women *into* development projects (Boserup, ==1970==).
WAD ★ | **Women and Development** (late 1970s): women are already in development, but on **unequal, exploitative** terms.
GAD ★ | **Gender and Development** (1980s): change unequal **power relations** between women and men.
Practical gender needs ★ | Immediate needs arising from women's current roles — water, food, health care (Moser, from Molyneux).
Strategic gender interests ★ | Long-term interests in **changing** women's position — land rights, laws against violence, political power.
Triple role | Women's **productive, reproductive and community-managing** work (Moser).
Empowerment ★ | Gaining the ability to make **strategic life choices** where it was denied (Kabeer, ==1999==).
Gender mainstreaming ★ | Assessing **every** policy for its effects on women and men (Beijing, ==1995==).
Gender budgeting | Analysing a government budget for its effects on women and men.
Structural Adjustment Programmes (SAPs) ★ | IMF/World Bank loan conditions from the 1980s — cut spending, privatise, liberalise trade.
Globalisation ★ | The growing **interdependence** of societies across the world (MCQ 2023).
Feminisation of labour | More women in paid work, often in **low-paid, insecure** jobs (Standing, ==1989==).
Global care chain | Poor women migrate to care for richer families' children, leaving their own children with others (Hochschild, ==2000==).
Export processing zone | An area where factories producing for export get tax and labour-law concessions.
Informal sector | Work outside labour laws and social security — home-based work, domestic work, street vending.
Unpaid care work | Cooking, cleaning, caring for children, the sick and the old, done without pay — mostly by women.
Feminist economics | Economics that counts **unpaid work** and studies how economies affect women and men differently.
Ecofeminism | Links the domination of **women** with the domination of **nature** (d'Eaubonne, ==1974==).
Feminist environmentalism | Women's link to nature comes from their **material work**, not biology (Agarwal, ==1992==).
GDI / GEM / GII | UNDP's gender indices: Gender Development Index and Gender Empowerment Measure (==1995==); Gender Inequality Index (==2010==).
MDGs / SDGs | Millennium Development Goals (==2000==, 8 goals) and Sustainable Development Goals (==2015==, 17 goals; **SDG 5** = gender equality).
Smart economics | The World Bank's argument that gender equality is good because it **raises growth** (WDR ==2012==).
:::

## 0.2  Development for whom?

::: example
In several African countries, rice-development projects gave new seeds, training, credit and machines to **men**, because planners assumed men were the farmers. In fact, **women** grew most of the rice. The men took over the land and the income; women lost their crops and their independence. The project "succeeded" in raising output — and made women poorer. Evidence of this kind — first set out systematically by the Danish economist **Ester Boserup** (==1970==) — began the whole field of gender and development.
:::

The lesson is that development is never **gender-neutral**. Every policy — a dam, a tax, a loan condition, a factory — lands on a society where men and women have different work, property, time and power. Gender analysis asks four questions of any development plan:

::: fig grid | Four questions gender analysis asks of any development plan.
@cols 4
Who does the work: paid and unpaid?
Who owns and controls: land, money, decisions?
Who benefits: income, services, power?
What changes: do gender relations become more equal?
:::

